We can simplify $F(p, q)$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
F(p, q) &= -2pq + 3p(1-q) + 3(1-p)q - 4(1-p)(1-q) \\
&= -2pq + 3p - 3pq + 3q - 4 + 4p + 4q - 4pq \\
&= 7p + 7q - 12pq - 4.
\end{align*}
To find the maximum value of $F(p, q)$ for a fixed value of $p$, we want to maximize the expression $7p + 7q - 12pq$.
Let $g(q) = 7p + 7q - 12pq$. To maximize $g(q)$, we take the derivative of $g(q)$ with respect to $q$ and set it equal to 0:
\begin{align*}
g'(q) &= 7 - 12p = 0 \\
12p &= 7 \\
p &= \frac{7}{12}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the value of $p$ that minimizes $G(p)$ is $\boxed{\frac{7}{12}}$.The answer is: \frac{7}{12}

