In a triangle, the sum of the lengths of any two sides must be greater than the length of the third side.
So, if the third side has length $x$ units, we have the inequalities:
\begin{align*}
33+x &> 42 \quad \text{(from the first side)} \\
42+x &> 33 \quad \text{(from the second side)} \\
x+33 &> 42 \quad \text{(from the third side)}
\end{align*}
Simplifying each inequality, we have:
\begin{align*}
x &> 9 \\
x &> -9 \\
x &> 9
\end{align*}
The smallest possible integer solution for $x$ is $x=10$.
Therefore, the least possible number of units in the perimeter of the triangle is $33+42+10 = \boxed{85}$.
The answer is: 85

